fund-js/24-authorization-and-tenant-scop.solution:
export function scopedRepo(db, tenantId) {
  const own = (id) => {
    const row = db.byId(id);
    return row && row.tenantId === tenantId ? row : null;
  };
  return {
    list() {
      return db.all().filter((row) => row.tenantId === tenantId);
    },
    get(id) {
      return own(id);
    },
    create(data) {
      return db.insert({ ...data, tenantId });
    },
    update(id, patch) {
      if (!own(id)) return null;
      const { tenantId: _tenantId, id: _id, ...fields } = patch;
      return db.update(id, fields);
    },
    remove(id) {
      return own(id) ? db.remove(id) : false;
    },
  };
}

fund-js/24-authorization-and-tenant-scop.explanation:
One ownership lookup, used by every by-id method:

```js
const own = (id) => {
  const row = db.byId(id);
  return row && row.tenantId === tenantId ? row : null;
};
// ...
create(data) { return db.insert({ ...data, tenantId }); },
update(id, patch) {
  if (!own(id)) return null;
  const { tenantId: _tenantId, id: _id, ...fields } = patch;
  return db.update(id, fields);
},
remove(id) { return own(id) ? db.remove(id) : false; },
```

In SQL the same idea is `WHERE id = ? AND tenant_id = ?` on every statement, UPDATE and DELETE included. Another tenant's row and a missing row give the same `null`, which the route turns into the same 404. In `create` the repository's tenant is spread LAST, so it wins over anything in the data.

**What the tests catch.** The plausible wrong answer scopes `list` and `get` and then lets `update` and `remove` go to the database by id: the read tests pass while another tenant's invoice is changed or deleted. Writing `{ tenantId, ...data }` lets the body choose the tenant, and passing the patch through untouched lets a caller move a row out of its tenant.

