const ALLOWED = {
  member: ['title', 'notes'],
  manager: ['title', 'notes', 'assigneeId', 'status'],
  admin: ['title', 'notes', 'assigneeId', 'status', 'ownerId'],
};

export function pickAllowed(body, role) {
  const picked = {};
  if (body === null || typeof body !== 'object' || !Object.hasOwn(ALLOWED, role)) return picked;
  for (const field of ALLOWED[role]) {
    if (Object.hasOwn(body, field)) picked[field] = body[field];
  }
  return picked;
}

fund-js/24-authorization-and-tenant-scop.explanation:
Let the role's list drive the copy, and take a field only when the body really has it:

```js
const picked = {};
if (body === null || typeof body !== 'object' || !Object.hasOwn(ALLOWED, role)) return picked;
for (const field of ALLOWED[role]) {
  if (Object.hasOwn(body, field)) picked[field] = body[field];
}
return picked;
```

Because the loop runs over YOUR list, a field you never listed cannot get through, whatever the client sends. `Object.hasOwn(ALLOWED, role)` matters as much as the field check: `ALLOWED['constructor']` is not `undefined`, it is the inherited `Object` function, so a plain lookup with a `?? []` fallback throws on that role instead of failing closed.

**What the tests catch.** The plausible wrong answer is a deny-list (copy the body, delete `role`, `tenantId` and `id`): `isAdmin`, `creditBalance` and `createdAt` sail through. Building the result with `Object.fromEntries` over the list invents `undefined` fields that a later update could write as NULL. Treating an unknown role as a member grants access that nobody gave, and skipping `null` values makes it impossible to clear a field.

