fund-js/24-authorization-and-tenant-scop.solution:
const ROLES = ['viewer', 'member', 'admin'];

export function can(user, action, resource) {
  if (!user || !resource) return false;
  if (!ROLES.includes(user.role)) return false;
  if (user.tenantId !== resource.tenantId) return false;
  const isAdmin = user.role === 'admin';
  const isOwner = user.id === resource.ownerId;
  switch (action) {
    case 'read':
      return true;
    case 'update':
      return isAdmin || (user.role === 'member' && isOwner);
    case 'delete':
      return isAdmin;
    default:
      return false;
  }
}

fund-js/24-authorization-and-tenant-scop.explanation:
Widest boundary first, then one line per action, then a refusal for everything else:

```js
if (!user || !resource) return false;
if (!ROLES.includes(user.role)) return false;
if (user.tenantId !== resource.tenantId) return false;
const isAdmin = user.role === 'admin';
const isOwner = user.id === resource.ownerId;
switch (action) {
  case 'read': return true;
  case 'update': return isAdmin || (user.role === 'member' && isOwner);
  case 'delete': return isAdmin;
  default: return false;
}
```

Because the tenant check runs before any role is looked at, no later line can grant access across tenants. The `default` branch is the deny-by-default rule: a new action stays closed until someone writes its line.

**What the tests catch.** The plausible wrong answer is "admins and owners may write" with the tenant compared only for reads: an admin of one tenant can update and delete another tenant's documents, and a member whose id matches `ownerId` in a foreign tenant can update there. A rule that lets only admins and owners read fails the must-be-allowed cases. Checking ownership without the role lets a viewer edit, and ending with `return true` allows every action nobody thought of.
